nodecode-optchat is a rewrite of aska-digital's aska-digital/optchat-memory:
https://github.com/aska-digital/optchat-memory
commit 7b0f3abd65b95996366d0495787c95af981ea587
MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 aska-digital (LICENSE)

The original is a Hermes Agent memory-provider plugin in Python: store.py keeps
an append-only JSONL log of every message, tree.py a binary summary tree,
view.py the budgeted tiling the agent reads, compact.py the background
summarizer, and provider.py the MemoryProvider ABC that wires them to the
harness. This rewrite carries the same four pieces as Lisp files of the same
names -- the log and its owner-only files, the node table and zoom, the view
and its due-pair merge, the pump and its COMPACT prompt -- and the same two
tools, optchat_zoom and optchat_date, as Nodecode tools, with the prompt the
cell's manual. It also adds what Nodecode has instead of a memory provider: a
:FRAME observer that writes each turn's messages as they are published, and
advice on the harness-section read, so the view rides every request as the
`optchat` block.

Left out, because the harness differs: the MemoryProvider ABC and its
per-profile home keying (Nodecode has one home and the cell takes a
`store_dir`); the POSIX/Windows file lock that keeps a second process off the
log (one Nodecode process owns the home); the five-try size-correction loop
around an over-long summary (the rewrite cuts at the budget); and the byte
exactness of the budget (characters here). The compactor's model call goes
through NLE:COMPLETE on the auxiliary target, the seam Hermes's
call_llm(task="optchat_compact") is on the other side of.
